#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_injective

#% text
# `function_injective(f, x)` gives `true` when `f` takes no value twice on its domain and `false` when it does.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A function whose derivative has one sign on its domain is injective; two equal values found among samples of the domain prove that it is not.

#% item
# `function_injective([f, cond], x)` restricts the domain.

#% item
# When neither is shown the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The exponential:

#% cell
function_injective(e^x, x)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A cubic with a local maximum and minimum:

#% cell
function_injective(x^3 - x, x)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The same cubic beyond its minimum:

#% cell
function_injective([x^3 - x, x > 1], x)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A parabola:

#% cell
function_injective(x^2, x)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
function_injective([x^2, x >= 0], x)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A function with a derivative of one sign is monotonic:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(e^x, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_monotonicity`, `function_sign`
